i='TCS-3399';_,c=card(i);refs=c['references'];refs[0]['locator']='Equation (1), Theorem 2 and Section 5, conjectured constant-factor Π₂ hardness.'
refs += [dict(id='solution',title='Linear discrepancy is Π₂-hard to approximate',authors='Pasin Manurangsi',year=2021,url='https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ipl.2021.106164',pdf_url='https://arxiv.org/pdf/2107.01235',locator='Information Processing Letters 172, article 106164; Theorem 1 and Lemma 5: Π₂ reduction with discrepancy ≤4/3 versus ≥3/2.'),dict(id='followup2026',title='Hardness of the Binary Covering Radius Problem in Large ℓp Norms',authors='Huck Bennett; Peter Ly',year=2026,url='https://doi.org/10.4230/LIPIcs.APPROX/RANDOM.2026.10',locator='Abstract; Section 1.1, Theorem 2; Section 1.2, linear discrepancy; Section 2.2, gap-problem definitions.')]
save(i,dict(title='Constant-factor Π₂ hardness of approximating linear discrepancy',formal='Historical conjecture, resolved affirmatively: is there an absolute constant γ>1 such that distinguishing lindisc(A)≤t from lindisc(A)>γt is Π₂^P-hard under deterministic polynomial-time many-one reductions, given a rational m×n matrix A and a positive rational threshold t? Here lindisc(A)=max_{w∈[0,1]ⁿ} min_{x∈{0,1}ⁿ} ‖A(w−x)‖∞. Manurangsi’s published theorem establishes the requested hardness for every fixed γ=9/8−ε with 0<ε<1/8. The source’s damaged fraction 32 referred to a 3/2 covering-radius comparison; its conjecture asks for analogous constant-factor hardness of linear discrepancy, not explicitly the identical 3/2 factor.',definitions='The matrix entries and t are encoded as binary rational numerators and positive denominators; both dimensions are part of the input and unbounded. For y∈Rᵐ, ‖y‖∞=max_{j∈[m]}|y_j|. A fractional vector w ranges over the entire real cube [0,1]ⁿ and x is a binary rounding of all its coordinates. Thus the maximization is outside the minimization: a different x may be chosen for each w. The maximum exists because the minimum of finitely many continuous functions of w is continuous on a compact set. This is not discrepancy at just w=(1/2,…,1/2), and the rounding vector does not range over all integer vectors as in lattice covering radius. The promise problem accepts instances with lindisc(A)≤t and rejects those with lindisc(A)>γt; behavior in the intervening gap is unrestricted. Π₂^P consists of languages decidable by a polynomial-time predicate with one polynomial-length universal binary quantifier followed by one polynomial-length existential binary quantifier. Hardness means that every language in this class has a deterministic polynomial-time map taking its yes instances into the low-discrepancy case and its no instances into the high-discrepancy case. The factor γ is fixed before the reduction and does not depend on the matrix dimensions. A γ-approximation to the value in the one-sided convention returns v with lindisc(A)≤v≤γ lindisc(A); the gap statement avoids ambiguity about approximation conventions.',question_type='yes_no',answer_criterion='The published Π₂ reduction resolves the constant-factor hardness conjecture: it has lindisc(A)≤4/3 in the yes case and lindisc(A)≥3/2 in the no case, giving hardness at every factor strictly below 9/8. This is an unconditional hardness theorem, not an unconditional proof that no polynomial-time algorithm exists. It does not establish hardness at factor 3/2, and no such stronger target is substituted for the source’s general conjecture.',context_blocks=[dict(title='Worst-case rounding error is the quantity being approximated',text='Linear discrepancy measures the largest, over fractional vectors, of the smallest achievable row-wise rounding error. Finding a useful rounding for one given vector and estimating this worst-case value are different computational tasks.',citation='primary'),dict(title='The source’s hardness question had a later direct answer',text='Li and Nikolov proved NP-hardness of exact evaluation and membership of the rational threshold problem in Π₂. They conjectured analogous constant-factor Π₂ approximation hardness to the lattice covering-radius problem. Manurangsi’s 2021 theorem explicitly answers that question.',citation='solution'),dict(title='The numerical factors belong to different problems',text='The source compares with a 3/2 covering-radius hardness result. The direct linear-discrepancy follow-up supplies a 9/8−ε factor. Its reduction has thresholds 4/3 and 3/2, whose ratio is 9/8; the occurrence of 3/2 as an absolute no-case threshold must not be mistaken for that approximation ratio.',citation='solution'),dict(title='A 2026 paper corroborates and extends the result',text='Bennett and Ly cite Manurangsi’s published Π₂ hardness for ℓ∞ linear discrepancy and extend it to a binary covering-radius promise problem. Their work also studies finite ℓp norms. This is later progress beyond a question already settled in 2021.',citation='followup2026')],why='Establishes that approximating the worst-case error of binary rounding captures the second universal level of the polynomial hierarchy, beyond ordinary NP-hardness.',references=refs,progress=[dict(date='2020',text='Exact evaluation is proved NP-hard and in Π₂; the source conjectures constant-factor Π₂ approximation hardness.',citation='primary'),dict(date='2021-12',text='Published Π₂-hardness at every factor 9/8−ε>1 resolves the conjecture.',citation='solution'),dict(date='2026',text='The binary covering-radius follow-up cites and strengthens the 2021 result while preserving the distinction between ℓ∞ and finite ℓp versions.',citation='followup2026')],status='resolved',status_note='Matched the original conjecture to Manurangsi’s Theorem 1 and the complete Lemma 5 reduction statement. Verified the journal publication metadata and read the September 2026 follow-up’s model and theorem. Resolution concerns some constant-factor Π₂ hardness, as explicitly claimed by the direct follow-up; no independent proof audit or proof-assistant certificate is claimed.',model_self_contained=True,requires_context=False,importance=dict(score=c['importance']['score'],method='editorial',reason='Classifies the hardness of the universal rounding-error objective, a fundamental discrepancy quantity with approximation-algorithm applications. Existing score retained.',assessed_on=DATE),review_note='Restored the truncated conjecture, corrected 32 to the contextual fraction 3/2, and separated it from the achieved factor 9/8−ε. Matched the direct published solution and the 2026 extension, then marked resolved.',working_summary=dict(sentences=['Linear discrepancy is the worst fractional vector’s smallest possible binary rounding error, measured in the maximum row norm.','The source conjectured that approximating this matrix quantity within some constant factor is Π₂-hard.','Manurangsi proved hardness at every factor strictly between 1 and 9/8.','The result was published in 2021 and is explicitly extended by a 2026 binary covering-radius paper.','The conjecture is resolved; the 3/2 factor mentioned in the original passage belongs to a covering-radius comparison and is not the achieved linear-discrepancy ratio.'],basis='saved_sources',source_basis='source-text',written_on=DATE,source_refs=['primary','solution','followup2026'])),notes=['Recovered linear discrepancy and the intended constant-factor Π₂ approximation conjecture.','Defined rational input, max–min order, norm, gap convention and reduction class.','Corrected damaged fraction and distinguished an absolute reduction threshold from an approximation factor.','Matched published direct solution and 2026 corroboration; marked resolved.'],checked_sources=[r['url'] for r in refs]+['https://research.google/pubs/linear-discrepancy-is-%CF%802-hard-to-approximate/'])
p=DIRECTORY/'queue.json';q=json.loads(p.read_text());_,c=card(i)
if not any(r['id']==i for r in q['dispositions']):q['dispositions'].append(dict(id=i,title=c['title'],state='resolved',reason=c['status_note'],card='data/cards/'+i+'.json'))
